This C program demonstrates a simple shell with support for executing commands and logging output to files. The user can input commands one at a time, and the program will fork a new process to execute each command. If the maximum number of processes has been reached, the command will be queued until a currently running process finishes. Additionally, the program handles SIGCHLD signals to manage child processes.

The log files are rotated when they reach a specified size (10 MB by default). A mutex is used to ensure that log file operations are thread-safe.

You can compile this program with the following command:
```bash
gcc -o shell_program shell_program.c -lpthread -lrt -ldl
```
Then run it with:
```bash
./shell_program
```
From there, you can enter commands and see their output in the log files. Press `exit` to close the program.']

